window.NTI=window.NTI||{};(window.NTI.docs=window.NTI.docs||{})["ansible-32-ansible-pdf-project-specification"]={"html":"<h1 id=\"ansible-pdf-project-specification\">ANSIBLE PDF PROJECT SPECIFICATION</h1>\n<h2 id=\"0-role-amp-mission\">0. ROLE &amp; MISSION</h2>\n<p>You are a technical documentation architect, DevOps curriculum designer, research orchestrator, visual information designer, and PDF publication engineer.</p>\n<p>Transform deeply researched Ansible learning material into a coherent, print-ready A4 technical documentation series rendered as standalone HTML and converted to PDF.</p>\n<p>The project is not a data-extraction exercise.</p>\n<p>The goal is to build a learning system that teaches Ansible from first principles, develops the learner's mental model progressively, and then connects Ansible to the surrounding DevOps ecosystem without turning the documentation into unrelated tutorials.</p>\n<p>The final publication must feel like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>an engineering manual</li>\n<li>a practical learning course</li>\n<li>a visual architecture guide</li>\n<li>a runbook</li>\n<li>a reference document</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It must NOT feel like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>copied vendor documentation</li>\n<li>search-result aggregation</li>\n<li>disconnected topic summaries</li>\n<li>a wall of Markdown converted to PDF</li>\n<li>a generic DevOps template with Ansible terminology swapped in</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"1-project-pipeline\">1. PROJECT PIPELINE</h1>\n<p>The project follows this exact lifecycle:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">SCOPE\n  ↓\nRESEARCH KEYWORD MAP\n  ↓\nWEB RESEARCH\n  ↓\nSOURCE VALIDATION\n  ↓\nCONCEPT / DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n  ↓\nECOSYSTEM MAPPING\n  ↓\nLEARNING ARCHITECTURE\n  ↓\nPDF COUNT DECISION\n  ↓\nCONTENT MARKDOWN\n  ↓\nASSET / ICON REGISTRY\n  ↓\nDIAGRAM PLAN\n  ↓\nPDF / THEME SPECIFICATION\n  ↓\nCONTENT REVIEW\n  ↓\nPDF 1 GENERATION\n  ↓\nVISUAL + TECHNICAL QA\n  ↓\nDESIGN FEEDBACK\n  ↓\nGLOBAL SPEC UPDATE\n  ↓\nREMAINING PDF GENERATION\n  ↓\nFULL-SERIES QA\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not collapse these phases into one generation step.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"2-milestone-rule\">2. MILESTONE RULE</h1>\n<h2 id=\"milestone-1-research-markdown-specifications\">Milestone 1: Research + Markdown + Specifications</h2>\n<p>Generate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>research files</li>\n<li>content Markdown files</li>\n<li>dependency maps</li>\n<li>source maps</li>\n<li>asset registry</li>\n<li>icon placement instructions</li>\n<li>diagram specifications</li>\n<li>PDF architecture</li>\n<li>theme specification</li>\n<li>layout specification</li>\n<li>pagination specification</li>\n<li>QA specification</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do NOT generate the complete PDF series at this milestone.</p>\n<p>The human reviews the Markdown and specifications first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"milestone-2-first-pdf\">Milestone 2: First PDF</h2>\n<p>Generate PDF 1 only.</p>\n<p>Use the first PDF as the design-validation artifact.</p>\n<h2 id=\"milestone-3-design-refinement\">Milestone 3: Design Refinement</h2>\n<p>Apply feedback from PDF 1 to the global specification, not only to one PDF.</p>\n<h2 id=\"milestone-4-final-series\">Milestone 4: Final Series</h2>\n<p>Generate the remaining PDFs.</p>\n<p>Then audit and improve the complete series.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"3-research-mission\">3. RESEARCH MISSION</h1>\n<p>Research Ansible from scratch using current, authoritative sources.</p>\n<p>The research process must determine what the learner actually needs to know, in what order, and why.</p>\n<p>Do not begin by assuming a chapter list.</p>\n<p>Discover the curriculum from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ansible architecture</li>\n<li>official terminology</li>\n<li>prerequisite concepts</li>\n<li>dependency relationships</li>\n<li>real-world usage</li>\n<li>production workflows</li>\n<li>common failure modes</li>\n<li>related DevOps technologies</li>\n<li>modern Ansible practices</li>\n<li>current version behavior</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary source priority:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Official Ansible documentation</li>\n<li>Official Ansible project repositories</li>\n<li>Official documentation for related technologies</li>\n<li>High-quality engineering documentation</li>\n<li>Reputable technical education resources</li>\n<li>Community material for practical edge cases and experience</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When conflicting sources exist:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify the conflict</li>\n<li>determine whether it is version-specific</li>\n<li>verify using primary documentation</li>\n<li>select the current technically correct behavior</li>\n<li>record important version assumptions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Never silently preserve obsolete tutorials.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"4-research-keyword-map\">4. RESEARCH KEYWORD MAP</h1>\n<p>Before writing content, create a complete keyword and concept map.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-1-core-ansible\">4.1 Core Ansible</h2>\n<p>Research, as applicable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ansible</li>\n<li>automation</li>\n<li>configuration management</li>\n<li>orchestration</li>\n<li>provisioning</li>\n<li>agentless architecture</li>\n<li>control node</li>\n<li>managed node</li>\n<li>inventory</li>\n<li>static inventory</li>\n<li>dynamic inventory</li>\n<li>host groups</li>\n<li>host patterns</li>\n<li>ad hoc commands</li>\n<li>playbook</li>\n<li>play</li>\n<li>task</li>\n<li>module</li>\n<li>module arguments</li>\n<li>FQCN</li>\n<li>collections</li>\n<li>roles</li>\n<li>variables</li>\n<li>variable precedence</li>\n<li>facts</li>\n<li>fact gathering</li>\n<li>registered variables</li>\n<li>templates</li>\n<li>Jinja2</li>\n<li>conditionals</li>\n<li>loops</li>\n<li>handlers</li>\n<li>blocks</li>\n<li>error handling</li>\n<li>retries</li>\n<li>until</li>\n<li>tags</li>\n<li>includes</li>\n<li>imports</li>\n<li>delegation</li>\n<li>local actions</li>\n<li>asynchronous tasks</li>\n<li>check mode</li>\n<li>diff mode</li>\n<li>idempotency</li>\n<li>become</li>\n<li>privilege escalation</li>\n<li>Vault</li>\n<li>credentials</li>\n<li>secrets</li>\n<li>configuration</li>\n<li>execution model</li>\n<li>YAML</li>\n<li>ansible.cfg</li>\n<li>CLI</li>\n<li>linting</li>\n<li>testing</li>\n<li>Molecule where relevant</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not assume every item must receive a full chapter.</p>\n<p>Determine its role in the learning system.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"5-related-technology-research\">5. RELATED-TECHNOLOGY RESEARCH</h1>\n<p>Research related technologies only where the relationship improves the Ansible mental model.</p>\n<p>Potential ecosystem topics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Linux</li>\n<li>SSH</li>\n<li>WinRM</li>\n<li>YAML</li>\n<li>Python</li>\n<li>Bash</li>\n<li>Git</li>\n<li>Docker</li>\n<li>Kubernetes</li>\n<li>Terraform</li>\n<li>OpenTofu</li>\n<li>Jenkins</li>\n<li>GitHub Actions</li>\n<li>GitLab CI</li>\n<li>AWS</li>\n<li>Azure</li>\n<li>GCP</li>\n<li>Packer</li>\n<li>Helm</li>\n<li>Vault</li>\n<li>Prometheus</li>\n<li>Grafana</li>\n<li>OpenTelemetry</li>\n<li>SRE</li>\n<li>DevOps</li>\n<li>CI/CD</li>\n<li>Infrastructure as Code</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The question is not:</p>\n<p>\"What is Terraform?\"</p>\n<p>The question is:</p>\n<p>\"Why would a DevOps engineer use Terraform alongside Ansible, and where does responsibility move from one tool to the other?\"</p>\n<p>The same principle applies to every related technology.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"6-ecosystem-boundary-rule\">6. ECOSYSTEM BOUNDARY RULE</h1>\n<p>Related tools must remain inside the Ansible story.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Terraform / OpenTofu\n    ↓\nProvision infrastructure\n    ↓\nAnsible\n    ↓\nConfigure operating systems and applications\n    ↓\nDocker\n    ↓\nPackage workloads\n    ↓\nKubernetes\n    ↓\nOrchestrate container workloads\n    ↓\nCI/CD\n    ↓\nTrigger automation\n    ↓\nObservability\n    ↓\nVerify real-world outcome\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not transform the Ansible series into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a Terraform course</li>\n<li>a Kubernetes course</li>\n<li>a Linux course</li>\n<li>a cloud certification guide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Explain enough context to make Ansible's role obvious.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"7-dependency-graph\">7. DEPENDENCY GRAPH</h1>\n<p>Create a dependency graph before defining the PDFs.</p>\n<p>The graph should answer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>what must be understood first?</li>\n<li>what concept causes the next concept to exist?</li>\n<li>what concepts are parallel?</li>\n<li>what concepts are optional?</li>\n<li>what concepts belong later because they rely on earlier mental models?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A starting mental model may resemble:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Linux / Remote Systems\n        ↓\nSSH / WinRM\n        ↓\nControl Node\n        ↓\nManaged Nodes\n        ↓\nInventory\n        ↓\nAd Hoc Commands\n        ↓\nPlaybooks\n        ↓\nPlays\n        ↓\nTasks\n        ↓\nModules\n        ↓\nVariables\n        ↓\nFacts / Registered Data\n        ↓\nConditionals / Loops\n        ↓\nTemplates\n        ↓\nHandlers\n        ↓\nRoles / Collections\n        ↓\nSecurity / Vault\n        ↓\nTesting / Linting\n        ↓\nCI/CD\n        ↓\nProduction Automation\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is a starting hypothesis only.</p>\n<p>Replace it when research shows a better instructional dependency graph.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"8-learning-arc\">8. LEARNING ARC</h1>\n<p>The complete series should progressively move from:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">WHY AUTOMATION?\n      ↓\nWHAT IS ANSIBLE?\n      ↓\nHOW DOES THE ARCHITECTURE WORK?\n      ↓\nHOW DOES ANSIBLE REACH MACHINES?\n      ↓\nHOW DO WE DESCRIBE TARGETS?\n      ↓\nHOW DO WE DECLARE DESIRED STATE?\n      ↓\nHOW DO VARIABLES AND DATA FLOW WORK?\n      ↓\nHOW DO WE ADD LOGIC?\n      ↓\nHOW DO WE ORGANIZE AUTOMATION?\n      ↓\nHOW DO WE SECURE IT?\n      ↓\nHOW DO WE TEST IT?\n      ↓\nHOW DOES IT ENTER CI/CD?\n      ↓\nHOW DOES IT OPERATE IN A REAL ENVIRONMENT?\n</code></pre>\n<p>The exact sequence may change after research.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"9-topic-handoff-rule\">9. TOPIC HANDOFF RULE</h1>\n<p>Every major topic must create a reason for the next topic.</p>\n<p>Avoid:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Inventory\n[page ends]\n\nVariables\n[new unrelated page]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Prefer:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Inventory tells Ansible WHICH machines to target.\n\nBut it does not tell those machines WHAT state they should reach.\n\nThat creates the need for playbooks.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then transition directly into the playbook model.</p>\n<p>Every major transition should use one or more of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a bridge paragraph</li>\n<li>a visual transition</li>\n<li>a recap</li>\n<li>a dependency diagram</li>\n<li>a practical problem that motivates the next concept</li>\n<li>a \"what we have / what is missing\" comparison</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The reader should understand why the next section exists.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"10-pdf-count\">10. PDF COUNT</h1>\n<p>Create no more than 3-4 PDFs for the Ansible series.</p>\n<p>Choose the count from information architecture, not arbitrary page limits.</p>\n<p>A possible structure is:</p>\n<h3 id=\"pdf-1\">PDF 1</h3>\n<p>Ansible Foundations</p>\n<ul>\n<li>automation problem</li>\n<li>architecture</li>\n<li>control node</li>\n<li>managed nodes</li>\n<li>transport</li>\n<li>inventory</li>\n<li>ad hoc commands</li>\n<li>YAML</li>\n<li>first playbook</li>\n<li>tasks</li>\n<li>modules</li>\n<li>idempotency</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pdf-2\">PDF 2</h3>\n<p>Playbook Engineering</p>\n<ul>\n<li>variables</li>\n<li>precedence</li>\n<li>facts</li>\n<li>registers</li>\n<li>conditionals</li>\n<li>loops</li>\n<li>templates</li>\n<li>handlers</li>\n<li>blocks</li>\n<li>error handling</li>\n<li>roles</li>\n<li>collections</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pdf-3\">PDF 3</h3>\n<p>Production Ansible</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vault</li>\n<li>credentials</li>\n<li>testing</li>\n<li>linting</li>\n<li>execution strategies</li>\n<li>project structure</li>\n<li>CI/CD</li>\n<li>inventories at scale</li>\n<li>real-world architecture</li>\n<li>troubleshooting</li>\n<li>operational practices</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pdf-4\">PDF 4</h3>\n<p>Advanced Automation / Integrated DevOps</p>\n<p>Only create this if research proves there is enough coherent advanced material that should not be forced into PDF 3.</p>\n<p>Do NOT create PDF 4 merely because the project has \"room for one more.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"11-content-schema\">11. CONTENT SCHEMA</h1>\n<p>Core concept books should use a coherent conceptual pattern.</p>\n<p>Where appropriate:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">WHAT\n↓\nWHY\n↓\nWHERE DOES IT FIT?\n↓\nHOW IT WORKS\n↓\nPRACTICAL EXAMPLE\n↓\nWHAT CAN FAIL\n↓\nREAL-WORLD CONNECTION\n↓\nINTERVIEW CHECK\n↓\nNEXT CONCEPT\n</code></pre>\n<p>These headings are a structural pattern, not a prison.</p>\n<p>For implementation-heavy topics, adapt the shape.</p>\n<p>For labs:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">OBJECTIVE\n↓\nPREREQUISITES\n↓\nARCHITECTURE\n↓\nSETUP\n↓\nSTEP\n↓\nOBSERVE\n↓\nEXECUTE\n↓\nVERIFY\n↓\nEXPLAIN\n↓\nFAILURE SCENARIOS\n↓\nCLEANUP\n↓\nPROOF\n</code></pre>\n<p>For references:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">COMMAND / SYNTAX\n↓\nPURPOSE\n↓\nEXAMPLE\n↓\nCOMMON FAILURE\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"12-standard-concept-explanation\">12. STANDARD CONCEPT EXPLANATION</h1>\n<p>Every important concept should answer:</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-it\">What is it?</h2>\n<p>Plain definition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-does-it-exist\">Why does it exist?</h2>\n<p>Problem it solves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-does-it-fit\">Where does it fit?</h2>\n<p>Its position in the automation workflow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-does-it-work\">How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Mechanics and internal flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes\">What changes?</h2>\n<p>What state or behavior is different after it runs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-can-fail\">What can fail?</h2>\n<p>Common failure modes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-should-i-care\">Why should I care?</h2>\n<p>Practical consequence.</p>\n<p>Not every small subtopic requires every subsection, but every major concept must have these questions answered somewhere in the learning flow.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"13-mental-model-requirement\">13. MENTAL-MODEL REQUIREMENT</h1>\n<p>Teach concepts as systems, not syntax fragments.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">USER\n  |\n  | ansible-playbook\n  v\nCONTROL NODE\n  |\n  | reads inventory\n  | selects hosts\n  | loads variables\n  | evaluates tasks\n  |\n  | SSH / WinRM\n  v\nMANAGED NODE\n  |\n  | module execution\n  v\nTARGET STATE\n  |\n  v\nRESULT\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then progressively explain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>what exists locally</li>\n<li>what is sent remotely</li>\n<li>what executes remotely</li>\n<li>what data comes back</li>\n<li>where idempotency matters</li>\n<li>how Ansible decides whether a task changed state</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The learner should understand the engine, not merely memorize YAML.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"14-command-explanation-standard\">14. COMMAND EXPLANATION STANDARD</h1>\n<p>For important commands use:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">COMMAND\n────────────────────────\nansible all -m ping\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then explain:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">MEANING\nWhat the command requests.\n\nTARGET\nWhich hosts are selected.\n\nINTERNAL FLOW\nWhat Ansible does internally.\n\nEXPECTED RESULT\nWhat should appear.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nWhat concept this proves.\n\nCOMMON FAILURE\nWhat can prevent success.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not explain every shell character.</p>\n<p>Focus on the Ansible behavior.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"15-yaml-explanation-standard\">15. YAML EXPLANATION STANDARD</h1>\n<p>Do not dump YAML.</p>\n<p>Use a real example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\"><span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">name:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">Configure</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">web</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">server</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">web</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">become:</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\">true</span>\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-attr\">name:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">Install</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">nginx</span>\n      <span class=\"hljs-attr\">ansible.builtin.package:</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">name:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">nginx</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-attr\">state:</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">present</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then show the hierarchy:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">PLAY\n |\n +-- hosts\n |\n +-- become\n |\n +-- tasks\n      |\n      +-- TASK\n           |\n           +-- MODULE\n           |\n           +-- ARGUMENTS\n</code></pre>\n<p>Explain the mapping between syntax and execution behavior.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"16-idpotency-visualization\">16. IDPOTENCY VISUALIZATION</h1>\n<p>Idempotency must be explained visually.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">FIRST RUN\n\nDesired state\n     ↓\nnginx absent\n     ↓\nInstall nginx\n     ↓\nCHANGED\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">SECOND RUN\n\nDesired state\n     ↓\nnginx already present\n     ↓\nNo action required\n     ↓\nOK\n</code></pre>\n<p>Make clear that:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Automation\n    ≠\n\"run this command again\"\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired state model is central.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"17-variables-and-data-flow\">17. VARIABLES AND DATA FLOW</h1>\n<p>Do not teach variables as syntax only.</p>\n<p>Show their movement:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Inventory variables\n       ↓\nGroup variables\n       ↓\nHost variables\n       ↓\nPlay variables\n       ↓\nTask variables\n       ↓\nRegistered data / facts\n       ↓\nTemplate / conditional / module\n</code></pre>\n<p>When variable precedence matters, teach it through an actual collision scenario.</p>\n<p>Avoid dumping a precedence table without first establishing why precedence exists.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"18-visual-system\">18. VISUAL SYSTEM</h1>\n<p>The visual language must reflect:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>infrastructure</li>\n<li>remote execution</li>\n<li>configuration</li>\n<li>desired state</li>\n<li>task execution</li>\n<li>state transitions</li>\n<li>automation loops</li>\n<li>environment boundaries</li>\n<li>security</li>\n<li>dependency flow</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Preferred visual vocabulary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>nodes</li>\n<li>connections</li>\n<li>arrows</li>\n<li>lanes</li>\n<li>execution paths</li>\n<li>terminal panels</li>\n<li>configuration panels</li>\n<li>state indicators</li>\n<li>architecture diagrams</li>\n<li>decision trees</li>\n<li>sequence diagrams</li>\n<li>dependency graphs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Avoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>generic SaaS dashboard aesthetics</li>\n<li>giant decorative icons</li>\n<li>excessive cards</li>\n<li>rainbow gradients</li>\n<li>childish illustrations</li>\n<li>decorative complexity without instructional purpose</li>\n<li>emoji-based navigation</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"19-icon-policy\">19. ICON POLICY</h1>\n<p>No emojis.</p>\n<p>Use real icon assets.</p>\n<p>For every meaningful icon:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>prefer official brand assets</li>\n<li>prefer SVG</li>\n<li>record source and license</li>\n<li>maintain consistent visual treatment</li>\n<li>avoid random icon-family mixing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Potential icons include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ansible</li>\n<li>Linux</li>\n<li>SSH</li>\n<li>Windows</li>\n<li>terminal</li>\n<li>server</li>\n<li>cloud</li>\n<li>Git</li>\n<li>Docker</li>\n<li>Kubernetes</li>\n<li>Terraform / OpenTofu</li>\n<li>Jenkins</li>\n<li>CI/CD</li>\n<li>security</li>\n<li>secrets</li>\n<li>database</li>\n<li>network</li>\n<li>YAML</li>\n<li>configuration</li>\n<li>deployment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only use an icon when it clarifies structure or meaning.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"20-icon-markers-in-markdown\">20. ICON MARKERS IN MARKDOWN</h1>\n<p>Every content Markdown file must explicitly identify important asset placement.</p>\n<p>Use:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">&lt;!-- icon: ansible --&gt;</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">&lt;!-- icon: ssh --&gt;</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>For contextual use:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">&lt;!-- icon: ssh | placement: architecture-diagram | purpose: transport path --&gt;</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>For major assets:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">&lt;!-- asset: diagram-control-to-managed-node --&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">&lt;!-- placement: after-section-introduction --&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">&lt;!-- purpose: explain agentless execution --&gt;</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>The PDF builder must not have to guess where visuals belong.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"21-asset-registry\">21. ASSET REGISTRY</h1>\n<p>Create:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">assets/asset-registry.md\n</code></pre>\n<p>For every asset track:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>License</th>\n<th>Used In</th>\n<th>Placement</th>\n<th>Fallback</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table></div>\n<p>The registry must cover icons, diagrams, logos, and external visual assets.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"22-diagram-registry\">22. DIAGRAM REGISTRY</h1>\n<p>Create:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">specs/diagram-spec.md\n</code></pre>\n<p>Each diagram should specify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ID</li>\n<li>title</li>\n<li>purpose</li>\n<li>source concept</li>\n<li>visual type</li>\n<li>nodes</li>\n<li>relationships</li>\n<li>labels</li>\n<li>direction</li>\n<li>color/state semantics</li>\n<li>approximate size</li>\n<li>placement</li>\n<li>whether it must remain on one page</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">ID:\nansible-control-flow\n\nPurpose:\nExplain the execution path from the user's command to the managed node.\n\nType:\nArchitecture + sequence hybrid\n\nNodes:\nUser\nControl Node\nInventory\nSSH\nManaged Node\nModule\nResult\n\nPlacement:\nImmediately after the first architecture explanation.\n\nConstraint:\nNever split across pages.\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"23-visual-explanation-rule\">23. VISUAL EXPLANATION RULE</h1>\n<p>Do not use visuals merely because the document \"needs images.\"</p>\n<p>A visual must explain one of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>structure</li>\n<li>flow</li>\n<li>comparison</li>\n<li>sequence</li>\n<li>decision</li>\n<li>state</li>\n<li>dependency</li>\n<li>failure</li>\n<li>recovery</li>\n<li>architecture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A diagram that merely decorates a page is a failed diagram.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"24-failure-model\">24. FAILURE MODEL</h1>\n<p>Failure analysis is a first-class learning mechanism.</p>\n<p>Important topics should include realistic failures such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>inventory mismatch</li>\n<li>unreachable host</li>\n<li>SSH authentication failure</li>\n<li>privilege escalation failure</li>\n<li>wrong module</li>\n<li>incorrect module arguments</li>\n<li>undefined variable</li>\n<li>variable precedence surprise</li>\n<li>Jinja rendering error</li>\n<li>template path issue</li>\n<li>handler not triggered</li>\n<li>permissions problem</li>\n<li>idempotency bug</li>\n<li>malformed YAML</li>\n<li>collection/module resolution issue</li>\n<li>Vault credential problem</li>\n<li>CI/CD environment mismatch</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For each major failure show:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">SYMPTOM\n↓\nLIKELY CAUSE\n↓\nHOW TO VERIFY\n↓\nFIX\n↓\nPREVENTION\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"25-real-world-architecture\">25. REAL-WORLD ARCHITECTURE</h1>\n<p>At least one major end-to-end architecture should connect:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Git Repository\n      ↓\nCI/CD\n      ↓\nAnsible Control Node\n      ↓\nInventory\n      ↓\nLinux / Windows Servers\n      ↓\nApplication Configuration\n      ↓\nContainers / Services\n      ↓\nObservability\n      ↓\nFeedback\n</code></pre>\n<p>Where relevant, show:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Terraform / OpenTofu\n        ↓\nInfrastructure\n        ↓\nAnsible\n        ↓\nConfiguration\n        ↓\nKubernetes / Application Platform\n</code></pre>\n<p>The purpose is to show where Ansible belongs in the larger system.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"26-lab-design\">26. LAB DESIGN</h1>\n<p>Labs must be experiments, not command lists.</p>\n<p>Recommended flow:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">OBSERVE\nWhat exists now?\n\nEXECUTE\nWhat action are we taking?\n\nWATCH\nWhat changes while it runs?\n\nVERIFY\nWhat proves success?\n\nEXPLAIN\nWhy did that happen?\n\nPROOF\nWhat concept did this experiment establish?\n</code></pre>\n<p>For each important command show:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">TAB / STEP\nCommand\nExpected change\nInternal Ansible action\nExpected output\nFailure interpretation\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not make a lab depend on unexplained magic.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"27-interview-exam-notes\">27. INTERVIEW / EXAM NOTES</h1>\n<p>Use interview material as a compact understanding check.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">INTERVIEW CHECK\n\nQ: Why is Ansible called agentless?\n\nA:\nThe managed node does not require a permanently\nrunning Ansible agent. Ansible normally connects\nfrom the control node using a supported transport,\nsuch as SSH or WinRM, and executes the required\nmodules or commands.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Keep these focused.</p>\n<p>Do not convert the documentation into a giant flashcard bank.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"28-content-file-architecture\">28. CONTENT FILE ARCHITECTURE</h1>\n<p>Use multiple Markdown files.</p>\n<p>Recommended starting structure:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">/research\n    research-plan.md\n    keyword-map.md\n    source-map.md\n    dependency-map.md\n    ecosystem-map.md\n\n/content\n    01-foundations.md\n    02-architecture.md\n    03-inventory-and-targeting.md\n    04-first-playbooks.md\n    05-modules-and-idempotency.md\n    06-variables-facts-registers.md\n    07-logic-and-data-flow.md\n    08-templates-handlers.md\n    09-roles-and-collections.md\n    10-security-and-vault.md\n    11-testing-and-quality.md\n    12-ci-cd-and-production.md\n    13-advanced-automation.md\n\n/specs\n    pdf-architecture.md\n    content-spec.md\n    visual-spec.md\n    theme-spec.md\n    typography-spec.md\n    icon-spec.md\n    diagram-spec.md\n    layout-spec.md\n    pagination-spec.md\n    qa-spec.md\n\n/assets\n    asset-registry.md\n\nREADME.md\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is a template, not a fixed outcome.</p>\n<p>Modify it after research.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"29-source-map\">29. SOURCE MAP</h1>\n<p>Create:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">research/source-map.md\n</code></pre>\n<p>Track:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Source Type</th>\n<th>Authority</th>\n<th>Date / Version</th>\n<th>Used For</th>\n<th>Confidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table></div>\n<p>Use the highest-authority source available.</p>\n<p>Do not use one blog as the source for the entire curriculum.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"30-content-source-discipline\">30. CONTENT SOURCE DISCIPLINE</h1>\n<p>The researched source material should be synthesized, not copied.</p>\n<p>Rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>paraphrase</li>\n<li>explain</li>\n<li>compare</li>\n<li>connect</li>\n<li>simplify where appropriate</li>\n<li>preserve technical accuracy</li>\n<li>cite important claims</li>\n<li>avoid long copied passages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The output must teach the concept rather than reproduce its documentation.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"31-pdf-design-system\">31. PDF DESIGN SYSTEM</h1>\n<p>The visual system should feel like an:</p>\n<p><strong>AUTOMATION CONTROL MANUAL</strong></p>\n<p>It can combine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>engineering manual</li>\n<li>infrastructure architecture document</li>\n<li>terminal/runbook aesthetic</li>\n<li>technical editorial publication</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The design must prioritize:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">CLARITY\n&gt;\nLEARNING FLOW\n&gt;\nREADABILITY\n&gt;\nINFORMATION DENSITY\n&gt;\nDECORATION\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"32-color-semantics\">32. COLOR SEMANTICS</h1>\n<p>Use a controlled technical palette.</p>\n<p>Recommended semantics:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Green   = success / converged / applied\nBlue    = information / execution / transport\nAmber   = warning / attention / manual decision\nRed     = failure / blocked / destructive action\nPurple  = data / artifact / packaged configuration\nBlack   = terminal / execution context\nGray    = neutral / inactive\n</code></pre>\n<p>Color must communicate state consistently.</p>\n<p>Never use color arbitrarily.</p>\n<p>Never rely on color alone.</p>\n<p>Pair important state with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>symbol</li>\n<li>label</li>\n<li>border</li>\n<li>text</li>\n</ul>\n<p>so printed grayscale remains understandable.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"33-typography\">33. TYPOGRAPHY</h1>\n<p>Preferred families:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-css\"><span class=\"hljs-attribute\">font-family</span>:\nInter,\n<span class=\"hljs-string\">\"Segoe UI\"</span>,\nArial,\nsans-serif;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Technical:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-css\"><span class=\"hljs-attribute\">font-family</span>:\n<span class=\"hljs-string\">\"SFMono-Regular\"</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-string\">\"Cascadia Code\"</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-string\">\"Consolas\"</span>,\nmonospace;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Baseline scale:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Document title       44–52px\nChapter title        30–36px\nSection title        17–20px\nBody                 11.5–13px\nMetadata             9–10px\nCode                 10–11px\nDiagram labels       9–11px\nCaptions             9–10px\n</code></pre>\n<p>Readability outranks page-count reduction.</p>\n<p>Never shrink the entire document to force content into fewer pages.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"34-page-geometry\">34. PAGE GEOMETRY</h1>\n<p>All pages are A4.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-css\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">@page</span> {\n    size: A4;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">margin</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.page</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">width</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">210mm</span>;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">min-height</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">297mm</span>;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">padding</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">16mm</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">16mm</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">19mm</span>;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">position</span>: relative;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">background</span>: <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">var</span>(--paper);\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">page-break-after</span>: always;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">overflow</span>: hidden;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Adjust values only when the visual system proves they need adjustment.</p>\n<p>Never allow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>clipping</li>\n<li>overflow</li>\n<li>content behind the footer</li>\n<li>diagram truncation</li>\n<li>broken page boundaries</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"35-page-header\">35. PAGE HEADER</h1>\n<p>Use a restrained technical header.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">ANSIBLE ENGINEERING\nCHAPTER 03\n\nINVENTORY &amp; TARGETING\n──────────────────────────────────────\nCONTROLLED HOST SELECTION\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>small uppercase identifier</li>\n<li>thin divider</li>\n<li>chapter title</li>\n<li>optional concept/category indicator</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Avoid oversized decorative headers.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"36-page-footer\">36. PAGE FOOTER</h1>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">ANSIBLE AUTOMATION MANUAL          CH 03\n────────────────────────────────────────\n03 / 19\n</code></pre>\n<p>Footer must remain subtle.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"37-cover\">37. COVER</h1>\n<p>Cover should visually communicate remote automation.</p>\n<p>Possible composition:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">CONTROL NODE\n      |\n      | SSH / WINRM\n      ↓\n┌───────────┐\n│ HOST 01   │\n└───────────┘\n      |\n      ├────────→ HOST 02\n      |\n      └────────→ HOST 03\n</code></pre>\n<p>Title:</p>\n<p><strong>ANSIBLE AUTOMATION</strong></p>\n<p>Subtitle:</p>\n<p><strong>From Remote Execution to Declarative Infrastructure</strong></p>\n<p>Additional metadata may include:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">AUTOMATION\nCONFIGURATION\nORCHESTRATION\n</code></pre>\n<p>No emojis.</p>\n<p>A creator/signature treatment may be retained from the reference publication system if desired, but it must remain secondary to the document title.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"38-chapter-openers\">38. CHAPTER OPENERS</h1>\n<p>Each chapter should begin with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>chapter number</li>\n<li>title</li>\n<li>short editorial framing</li>\n<li>concept/lifecycle visual</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">03\n\nINVENTORY\n\nAnsible cannot automate a machine\nuntil it knows which machine exists.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">INVENTORY\n     ↓\nHOST SELECTION\n     ↓\nCONNECTION\n     ↓\nTASK EXECUTION\n</code></pre>\n<p>The opener should establish the chapter's role.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"39-architecture-component\">39. ARCHITECTURE COMPONENT</h1>\n<p>Create reusable architecture blocks.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">┌───────────────────┐\n│ CONTROL NODE      │\n│                   │\n│ ansible-playbook  │\n│ inventory         │\n│ variables         │\n│ modules           │\n└─────────┬─────────┘\n          │\n       SSH / WinRM\n          │\n ┌────────┴─────────┐\n ↓                  ↓\nHOST 01            HOST 02\nLinux              Windows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use real HTML/CSS diagrams rather than raw ASCII in final PDFs.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"40-execution-flow\">40. EXECUTION FLOW</h1>\n<p>The core execution pattern should be visualized repeatedly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">COMMAND\n   ↓\nLOAD CONFIG\n   ↓\nLOAD INVENTORY\n   ↓\nSELECT HOSTS\n   ↓\nLOAD VARIABLES / FACTS\n   ↓\nPROCESS PLAY\n   ↓\nEXECUTE TASK\n   ↓\nRUN MODULE\n   ↓\nRETURN RESULT\n   ↓\nNEXT TASK\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is one of the main visual anchors of the series.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"41-state-idempotency-component\">41. STATE / IDEMPOTENCY COMPONENT</h1>\n<p>Represent result states explicitly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">OK\nCHANGED\nFAILED\nSKIPPED\nUNREACHABLE\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not communicate state only through color.</p>\n<p>Use symbols and labels.</p>\n<p>For configuration state, show:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">DESIRED STATE\n      ↓\nCURRENT STATE\n      ↓\nCOMPARE\n      ↓\nCHANGE OR NO-OP\n      ↓\nRESULT\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"42-terminal-design\">42. TERMINAL DESIGN</h1>\n<p>Terminal blocks should resemble real engineering consoles.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-css\"><span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.terminal</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">background</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">#111614</span>;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">color</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">#e8eeeb</span>;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">border-radius</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">8px</span>;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">padding</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">14px</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">16px</span>;\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">font</span>: <span class=\"hljs-number\">10.5px</span>/<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.65</span>\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">\"Cascadia Code\"</span>,\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">\"Consolas\"</span>,\n        monospace;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">$ ansible web -m ping\n\nweb-01  SUCCESS\nweb-02  SUCCESS\nweb-03  SUCCESS\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not overdecorate terminal blocks.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"43-command-result-pattern\">43. COMMAND + RESULT PATTERN</h1>\n<p>Use:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">COMMAND\n────────────────────────\n\n$ ansible all -m ping\n\nRESULT\n────────────────────────\n\nhost01 | SUCCESS =&gt; {\n    \"changed\": false,\n    \"ping\": \"pong\"\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then explain:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">WHAT HAPPENED\nWHY IT MATTERS\nCOMMON FAILURE\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"44-comparison-component\">44. COMPARISON COMPONENT</h1>\n<p>Use compact comparison tables for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ansible vs Terraform</li>\n<li>Ansible vs Kubernetes</li>\n<li>Ansible vs shell scripts</li>\n<li>Ansible vs cloud-init</li>\n<li>configuration management vs provisioning</li>\n<li>push vs pull concepts</li>\n<li>ad hoc commands vs playbooks</li>\n<li>role vs collection</li>\n<li>import vs include</li>\n<li>variable vs fact</li>\n<li>handler vs normal task</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">                     TERRAFORM        ANSIBLE\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────\nPrimary concern      Infrastructure   Configuration\nState model          Desired infra    Desired config\nTypical target       Cloud resources  Hosts/apps\nTypical result       Resources         Configured systems\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not turn comparisons into technology tutorials.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"45-decision-diagrams\">45. DECISION DIAGRAMS</h1>\n<p>When there is a practical choice, use decision diagrams.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">WHAT ARE YOU AUTOMATING?\n          |\n     ┌────┴─────┐\n     ↓          ↓\nINFRASTRUCTURE  HOST / APP CONFIG\n     |                |\n     ↓                ↓\nTerraform/OpenTofu   Ansible\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use decision trees when they reduce ambiguity.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"46-why-this-exists-callout\">46. WHY-THIS-EXISTS CALLOUT</h1>\n<p>Use compact editorial callouts.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">WHY THIS EXISTS\n\nA shell script can tell a machine what command\nto run.\n\nA repeatable automation system must also define\nwhich machines are targeted, what state is desired,\nand what should happen when the desired state\nalready exists.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use a restrained accent border.</p>\n<p>Do not turn every paragraph into a card.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"47-gotcha-callout\">47. GOTCHA CALLOUT</h1>\n<p>Use meaningful warnings.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">GOTCHA\n\n\"OK\" does not mean Ansible changed nothing.\n\nA task can legitimately finish with a successful\nresult after changing the target system.\n\nAlways read the result state.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Warnings must teach something useful.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"48-pagination-rules\">48. PAGINATION RULES</h1>\n<p>Pagination is part of content design.</p>\n<p>Never split:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>architecture diagrams</li>\n<li>code blocks</li>\n<li>command/result pairs</li>\n<li>tables</li>\n<li>callouts</li>\n<li>visual explanations</li>\n<li>question/answer pairs</li>\n<li>heading + opening paragraph</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-css\"><span class=\"hljs-selector-tag\">h1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-selector-tag\">h2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-selector-tag\">h3</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">break-after</span>: avoid;\n}\n\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.callout</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.diagram</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.code-block</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.table-container</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.terminal</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.cmdres</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.architecture</span>,\n<span class=\"hljs-selector-class\">.comparison</span> {\n    <span class=\"hljs-attribute\">break-inside</span>: avoid;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Apply <code>break-inside: avoid</code> only to reasonably sized units.</p>\n<p>Never put it on giant containers that create large blank holes.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"49-page-density\">49. PAGE DENSITY</h1>\n<p>Use page space intelligently.</p>\n<p>Target:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">8–12 meaningful content blocks per page\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is a guideline, not a mathematical requirement.</p>\n<p>Never:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>add filler</li>\n<li>duplicate explanations</li>\n<li>stretch content artificially</li>\n<li>enlarge whitespace with arbitrary padding</li>\n<li>shrink fonts excessively</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If meaningful content naturally needs another page, create another page.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"50-page-space-optimization\">50. PAGE SPACE OPTIMIZATION</h1>\n<p>Priority order when a page is too dense:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reorganize content.</li>\n<li>Remove redundant wording.</li>\n<li>Move a complete semantic unit.</li>\n<li>Recompose or resize the visual.</li>\n<li>Adjust spacing modestly.</li>\n<li>Adjust typography modestly.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Never solve layout problems by making text microscopic.</p>\n<p>When a page contains excessive blank space:</p>\n<p>Use meaningful visual elements only when they improve understanding.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"51-diagram-safety\">51. DIAGRAM SAFETY</h1>\n<p>Every diagram must be checked for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>clipping</li>\n<li>overflow</li>\n<li>unreadable labels</li>\n<li>confusing arrows</li>\n<li>disconnected nodes</li>\n<li>poor hierarchy</li>\n<li>accidental page split</li>\n<li>text collision</li>\n<li>excessive density</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A diagram must make sense without the surrounding paragraph.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"52-section-continuity\">52. SECTION CONTINUITY</h1>\n<p>Use references such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">We now know how Ansible identifies its targets.\n\nBut selecting a host does not define the state\nwe want that host to reach.\n\nThat is the job of the playbook.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Continuity is a requirement across the entire series.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"53-reference-learning-balance\">53. REFERENCE + LEARNING BALANCE</h1>\n<p>The publication serves two purposes:</p>\n<h3 id=\"learning\">Learning</h3>\n<p>Explains concepts progressively.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reference\">Reference</h3>\n<p>Allows the learner to return later and quickly locate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>commands</li>\n<li>syntax</li>\n<li>failure modes</li>\n<li>architecture</li>\n<li>terminology</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not sacrifice the learning path to become a reference dump.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"54-pdf-structure-audit\">54. PDF STRUCTURE AUDIT</h1>\n<p>Before final generation, run a structure audit over every content file.</p>\n<p>For core concept books detect:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">WHAT\nWHY\nWHEN / WHERE\nHOW\nFAILURE MODES\nINTERVIEW\nICON MARKERS\n</code></pre>\n<p>For labs detect:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">OBJECTIVE\nPREREQUISITES\nARCHITECTURE\nSETUP\nSTEPS\nOBSERVE\nEXECUTE\nVERIFY\nFAILURE\nCLEANUP\nPROOF\n</code></pre>\n<p>For references detect:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">COMMAND / SYNTAX\nPURPOSE\nEXAMPLE\nFAILURE\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not force every genre into one structure.</p>\n<p>Record differences explicitly.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"55-structure-audit-table\">55. STRUCTURE AUDIT TABLE</h1>\n<p>Create an audit table:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ID</th>\n<th>Title</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>WHAT</th>\n<th>WHY</th>\n<th>WHEN</th>\n<th>HOW</th>\n<th>FAIL</th>\n<th>Interview</th>\n<th>Icons</th>\n<th>Words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table></div>\n<p>Use the audit as a quality signal, not a blind pass/fail mechanism.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"56-audit-interpretation\">56. AUDIT INTERPRETATION</h1>\n<p>A missing structure element can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a genuine defect</li>\n<li>intentionally omitted because the genre differs</li>\n<li>replaced by a stronger equivalent heading</li>\n<li>unnecessary for a reference/lab document</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Document the reason.</p>\n<p>Do not mechanically \"fix\" every missing heading.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"57-word-count\">57. WORD COUNT</h1>\n<p>Word count is diagnostic only.</p>\n<p>Use it to identify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>under-covered concepts</li>\n<li>suspiciously short chapters</li>\n<li>unexpectedly massive chapters</li>\n<li>duplicated material</li>\n<li>research gaps</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not optimize for a specific word count.</p>\n<p>A 300-word concept may be complete.</p>\n<p>A 1,500-word concept may still be badly explained.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"58-icon-audit\">58. ICON AUDIT</h1>\n<p>Every content <code>.md</code> file should contain appropriate icon markers where visuals are beneficial.</p>\n<p>The audit must report:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>zero markers</li>\n<li>unusually high marker counts</li>\n<li>missing expected assets</li>\n<li>duplicate asset use</li>\n<li>inconsistent asset naming</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not add icons merely to satisfy a numeric target.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"59-source-version-audit\">59. SOURCE / VERSION AUDIT</h1>\n<p>For version-sensitive material, verify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>current installation guidance</li>\n<li>current CLI syntax</li>\n<li>current collection/module naming</li>\n<li>current project terminology</li>\n<li>current security practices</li>\n<li>supported transports</li>\n<li>current recommendations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Record version assumptions.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"60-final-pdf-qa\">60. FINAL PDF QA</h1>\n<p>Every PDF must go through:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">GENERATE\n   ↓\nRENDER\n   ↓\nPAGE-BY-PAGE VISUAL INSPECTION\n   ↓\nTECHNICAL CONTENT INSPECTION\n   ↓\nLAYOUT AUDIT\n   ↓\nFIX\n   ↓\nRENDER AGAIN\n   ↓\nFINAL REVIEW\n</code></pre>\n<p>Never declare success because the file opens.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"61-qa-checklist\">61. QA CHECKLIST</h1>\n<p>Verify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>complete HTML document</li>\n<li>no raw Markdown</li>\n<li>no raw ASCII diagrams in final presentation</li>\n<li>all diagrams rebuilt as real HTML/CSS or approved vector assets</li>\n<li>all code readable</li>\n<li>tables styled correctly</li>\n<li>icons render correctly</li>\n<li>no broken asset paths</li>\n<li>no missing glyphs</li>\n<li>no clipping</li>\n<li>no overlap</li>\n<li>no diagram overflow</li>\n<li>no orphaned headings</li>\n<li>no broken command/result pairs</li>\n<li>no split comparison tables</li>\n<li>no accidental empty pages</li>\n<li>no giant unexplained whitespace</li>\n<li>consistent typography</li>\n<li>consistent spacing</li>\n<li>consistent terminology</li>\n<li>coherent chapter sequence</li>\n<li>coherent transitions</li>\n<li>correct page numbering</li>\n<li>A4 print correctness</li>\n<li>background graphics supported in print settings</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"62-content-qa\">62. CONTENT QA</h1>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<h3 id=\"technical-reviewer\">Technical reviewer</h3>\n<p>Is the information current and technically correct?</p>\n<h3 id=\"beginner-reviewer\">Beginner reviewer</h3>\n<p>Could someone starting from zero understand the concept?</p>\n<h3 id=\"practitioner-reviewer\">Practitioner reviewer</h3>\n<p>Does the material resemble real DevOps usage?</p>\n<h3 id=\"visual-reviewer\">Visual reviewer</h3>\n<p>Does the layout teach efficiently?</p>\n<h3 id=\"curriculum-reviewer\">Curriculum reviewer</h3>\n<p>Does each topic arrive at the right point in the dependency chain?</p>\n<p>Any serious failure must be fixed.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"63-final-content-principles\">63. FINAL CONTENT PRINCIPLES</h1>\n<p>The final series must optimize:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">UNDERSTANDING\n    &gt;\nACCURACY\n    &gt;\nCOHERENCE\n    &gt;\nVISUAL COMMUNICATION\n    &gt;\nREADABILITY\n    &gt;\nCOMPLETENESS\n</code></pre>\n<p>Completeness does not mean including everything Ansible can do.</p>\n<p>Include what is necessary to develop:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a strong mental model</li>\n<li>practical working ability</li>\n<li>troubleshooting ability</li>\n<li>architectural understanding</li>\n<li>awareness of production concerns</li>\n<li>understanding of Ansible's place in DevOps</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"64-stop-condition-before-pdf-generation\">64. STOP CONDITION BEFORE PDF GENERATION</h1>\n<p>When the first milestone is complete, stop.</p>\n<p>Return:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">RESEARCH\n    ↓\nCONTENT MD\n    ↓\nSPECS\n    ↓\nASSET REGISTRY\n    ↓\nDIAGRAM REGISTRY\n    ↓\nAUDIT REPORT\n</code></pre>\n<p>Do not generate all PDFs yet.</p>\n<p>The content and architecture are reviewed first.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"65-final-delivery-contract\">65. FINAL DELIVERY CONTRACT</h1>\n<p>The completed project must have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">/research\n/content\n/specs\n/assets\nREADME.md\n</code></pre>\n<p>The PDF generator must consume these artifacts rather than inventing missing structure during rendering.</p>\n<p>The specification is the source of truth.</p>\n<p>The Markdown contains the content.</p>\n<p>The asset registry contains the visual dependencies.</p>\n<p>The PDF builder converts the approved system into the final publication.</p>\n<p>Never let the renderer silently redesign the curriculum.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1 id=\"66-non-negotiable-rule\">66. NON-NEGOTIABLE RULE</h1>\n<p>Do not produce a \"pretty PDF.\"</p>\n<p>Produce a document in which:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">THE RESEARCH\n      ↓\nDRIVES THE CONTENT\n      ↓\nDRIVES THE INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE\n      ↓\nDRIVES THE VISUALS\n      ↓\nDRIVES THE PAGE COMPOSITION\n      ↓\nDRIVES THE FINAL PDF\n</code></pre>\n<p>Every layer must support the layer after it.</p>\n<p>The final result should feel intentionally engineered from the learner's mental model outward.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"ansible-pdf-project-specification","text":"ANSIBLE PDF PROJECT SPECIFICATION","level":1},{"id":"0-role-amp-mission","text":"0. ROLE &amp; MISSION","level":2},{"id":"1-project-pipeline","text":"1. PROJECT PIPELINE","level":1},{"id":"2-milestone-rule","text":"2. MILESTONE RULE","level":1},{"id":"milestone-1-research-markdown-specifications","text":"Milestone 1: Research + Markdown + Specifications","level":2},{"id":"milestone-2-first-pdf","text":"Milestone 2: First PDF","level":2},{"id":"milestone-3-design-refinement","text":"Milestone 3: Design Refinement","level":2},{"id":"milestone-4-final-series","text":"Milestone 4: Final Series","level":2},{"id":"3-research-mission","text":"3. RESEARCH MISSION","level":1},{"id":"4-research-keyword-map","text":"4. RESEARCH KEYWORD MAP","level":1},{"id":"4-1-core-ansible","text":"4.1 Core Ansible","level":2},{"id":"5-related-technology-research","text":"5. RELATED-TECHNOLOGY RESEARCH","level":1},{"id":"6-ecosystem-boundary-rule","text":"6. ECOSYSTEM BOUNDARY RULE","level":1},{"id":"7-dependency-graph","text":"7. DEPENDENCY GRAPH","level":1},{"id":"8-learning-arc","text":"8. LEARNING ARC","level":1},{"id":"9-topic-handoff-rule","text":"9. TOPIC HANDOFF RULE","level":1},{"id":"10-pdf-count","text":"10. PDF COUNT","level":1},{"id":"pdf-1","text":"PDF 1","level":3},{"id":"pdf-2","text":"PDF 2","level":3},{"id":"pdf-3","text":"PDF 3","level":3},{"id":"pdf-4","text":"PDF 4","level":3},{"id":"11-content-schema","text":"11. CONTENT SCHEMA","level":1},{"id":"12-standard-concept-explanation","text":"12. STANDARD CONCEPT EXPLANATION","level":1},{"id":"what-is-it","text":"What is it?","level":2},{"id":"why-does-it-exist","text":"Why does it exist?","level":2},{"id":"where-does-it-fit","text":"Where does it fit?","level":2},{"id":"how-does-it-work","text":"How does it work?","level":2},{"id":"what-changes","text":"What changes?","level":2},{"id":"what-can-fail","text":"What can fail?","level":2},{"id":"why-should-i-care","text":"Why should I care?","level":2},{"id":"13-mental-model-requirement","text":"13. MENTAL-MODEL REQUIREMENT","level":1},{"id":"14-command-explanation-standard","text":"14. COMMAND EXPLANATION STANDARD","level":1},{"id":"15-yaml-explanation-standard","text":"15. YAML EXPLANATION STANDARD","level":1},{"id":"16-idpotency-visualization","text":"16. IDPOTENCY VISUALIZATION","level":1},{"id":"17-variables-and-data-flow","text":"17. VARIABLES AND DATA FLOW","level":1},{"id":"18-visual-system","text":"18. VISUAL SYSTEM","level":1},{"id":"19-icon-policy","text":"19. ICON POLICY","level":1},{"id":"20-icon-markers-in-markdown","text":"20. ICON MARKERS IN MARKDOWN","level":1},{"id":"21-asset-registry","text":"21. ASSET REGISTRY","level":1},{"id":"22-diagram-registry","text":"22. DIAGRAM REGISTRY","level":1},{"id":"23-visual-explanation-rule","text":"23. VISUAL EXPLANATION RULE","level":1},{"id":"24-failure-model","text":"24. FAILURE MODEL","level":1},{"id":"25-real-world-architecture","text":"25. REAL-WORLD ARCHITECTURE","level":1},{"id":"26-lab-design","text":"26. LAB DESIGN","level":1},{"id":"27-interview-exam-notes","text":"27. INTERVIEW / EXAM NOTES","level":1},{"id":"28-content-file-architecture","text":"28. CONTENT FILE ARCHITECTURE","level":1},{"id":"29-source-map","text":"29. SOURCE MAP","level":1},{"id":"30-content-source-discipline","text":"30. CONTENT SOURCE DISCIPLINE","level":1},{"id":"31-pdf-design-system","text":"31. PDF DESIGN SYSTEM","level":1},{"id":"32-color-semantics","text":"32. COLOR SEMANTICS","level":1},{"id":"33-typography","text":"33. TYPOGRAPHY","level":1},{"id":"34-page-geometry","text":"34. PAGE GEOMETRY","level":1},{"id":"35-page-header","text":"35. PAGE HEADER","level":1},{"id":"36-page-footer","text":"36. PAGE FOOTER","level":1},{"id":"37-cover","text":"37. COVER","level":1},{"id":"38-chapter-openers","text":"38. CHAPTER OPENERS","level":1},{"id":"39-architecture-component","text":"39. ARCHITECTURE COMPONENT","level":1},{"id":"40-execution-flow","text":"40. EXECUTION FLOW","level":1},{"id":"41-state-idempotency-component","text":"41. STATE / IDEMPOTENCY COMPONENT","level":1},{"id":"42-terminal-design","text":"42. TERMINAL DESIGN","level":1},{"id":"43-command-result-pattern","text":"43. COMMAND + RESULT PATTERN","level":1},{"id":"44-comparison-component","text":"44. COMPARISON COMPONENT","level":1},{"id":"45-decision-diagrams","text":"45. DECISION DIAGRAMS","level":1},{"id":"46-why-this-exists-callout","text":"46. WHY-THIS-EXISTS CALLOUT","level":1},{"id":"47-gotcha-callout","text":"47. GOTCHA CALLOUT","level":1},{"id":"48-pagination-rules","text":"48. PAGINATION RULES","level":1},{"id":"49-page-density","text":"49. PAGE DENSITY","level":1},{"id":"50-page-space-optimization","text":"50. PAGE SPACE OPTIMIZATION","level":1},{"id":"51-diagram-safety","text":"51. DIAGRAM SAFETY","level":1},{"id":"52-section-continuity","text":"52. SECTION CONTINUITY","level":1},{"id":"53-reference-learning-balance","text":"53. REFERENCE + LEARNING BALANCE","level":1},{"id":"learning","text":"Learning","level":3},{"id":"reference","text":"Reference","level":3},{"id":"54-pdf-structure-audit","text":"54. PDF STRUCTURE AUDIT","level":1},{"id":"55-structure-audit-table","text":"55. STRUCTURE AUDIT TABLE","level":1},{"id":"56-audit-interpretation","text":"56. AUDIT INTERPRETATION","level":1},{"id":"57-word-count","text":"57. WORD COUNT","level":1},{"id":"58-icon-audit","text":"58. ICON AUDIT","level":1},{"id":"59-source-version-audit","text":"59. SOURCE / VERSION AUDIT","level":1},{"id":"60-final-pdf-qa","text":"60. FINAL PDF QA","level":1},{"id":"61-qa-checklist","text":"61. QA CHECKLIST","level":1},{"id":"62-content-qa","text":"62. CONTENT QA","level":1},{"id":"technical-reviewer","text":"Technical reviewer","level":3},{"id":"beginner-reviewer","text":"Beginner reviewer","level":3},{"id":"practitioner-reviewer","text":"Practitioner reviewer","level":3},{"id":"visual-reviewer","text":"Visual reviewer","level":3},{"id":"curriculum-reviewer","text":"Curriculum reviewer","level":3},{"id":"63-final-content-principles","text":"63. FINAL CONTENT PRINCIPLES","level":1},{"id":"64-stop-condition-before-pdf-generation","text":"64. STOP CONDITION BEFORE PDF GENERATION","level":1},{"id":"65-final-delivery-contract","text":"65. FINAL DELIVERY CONTRACT","level":1},{"id":"66-non-negotiable-rule","text":"66. NON-NEGOTIABLE RULE","level":1}],"words":3417,"assets":[]};